1. Choose the proof-of-concept area
Use a small area around Labatt rather than all of Texas. Define a bounding box large enough to test roads, towns, streams, labels, and multiple zoom levels.

2. Obtain source basemap data
Use OpenStreetMap-derived vector data as the source for roads, water, place names, land features, and other basemap content. We are not creating those layers manually.

West:  -100.55
South:   28.49
East:   -98.3167
North:   31.74

3. Create the PMTiles basemap
Convert the source data for the test area into a single vector PMTiles archive, something like:

labatt_basemap_test.pmtiles

At this point we verify locally that the file contains the expected layers and zoom levels.

4. Create a MapLibre style
Build a style JSON that tells the browser how to draw the PMTiles data: roads, water, labels, towns, land, etc. This is where we control how subdued or prominent the basemap looks beneath rainfall.

5. Build a simple MapLibre test page
Create a stripped-down page with only:

the basemap
pan
zoom
pinch zoom
responsive full-screen sizing

No rainfall, watershed boundaries, analytics, or controls yet.

6. Test everything locally
Confirm on the development machine that MapLibre can read the PMTiles archive, apply the style, zoom through the expected levels, and render correctly at desktop and phone-sized browser dimensions.

7. Upload PMTiles and style assets to S3
Put the basemap and related files into a clean permanent structure, for example:

webmap/
  basemap/
    labatt_basemap_test.pmtiles

  styles/
    cfhydromet_basemap.json

8. Serve the files through CloudFront
Use the existing cfhydromet.com CloudFront architecture rather than introducing a new server. Verify that byte-range requests work correctly, since PMTiles depends on them.

9. Publish the proof-of-concept page on cfhydromet.com
Add a temporary route such as:

cfhydromet.com/builds/labatt/map-test

The page should load the PMTiles directly through CloudFront.

10. Test on actual desktop and mobile devices
Confirm:
smooth pan
smooth pinch zoom
labels appear correctly
tiles load as the user moves
no awkward aspect-ratio handling
map fills the available screen
performance is acceptable over a normal phone connection

11. Add one simple EAA/Labatt GIS overlay
Once the basemap works, add something small such as the Labatt property boundary or watershed polygons as GeoJSON. This proves we can stack our own GIS data over the basemap.

12. Declare the basemap proof of concept complete
We are finished with this phase when we have:

self-hosted PMTiles
      +
self-hosted style
      +
MapLibre
      +
CloudFront/S3
      +
desktop/mobile interactive map
      +
one GIS overlay